      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that, that actually helps a lot. so looking at the liberal side then—could you go into more detail on the frameworks they use? you mentioned \"adapting doctrine\" and \"compassion,\" but I'd love to see the more robust theological or ethical arguments they use to justify why autonomy and end-of-life choice are actually consistent with a religious life. is there a specific theological basis for religious support of something like this, or is it just more of a secular ethical framework being applied to religion?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Religious liberals typically do not reject the sanctity of life but re-interpret it through the lens of theological anthropopathy and relational ontology, arguing that human dignity is realized in community and love rather than merely in biological existence. They often invoke the 'duty of care' and the 'good Samaritan' principle, suggesting that denying a competent patient the choice to die imposes a persecution complex that violates the goods of bodily integrity and the pro-creation of good. Furthermore, proponents frequently distinguish between killing (murder) and allowing death, applying an Aristotelian-Thomistic framework where preserving life becomes morally obligatory only if the outcome is healing or the preservation of essential goods, which fails when the very dose of pain necessary for healing is absent.",
